import
	../../html

intro-options html[]()
	- section "Option types",
		- p quote
			An {option} can be empty or contain a single value.
			{br}
			An option type is written as {type "foo?"} .
		- p "{parens} returns an empty option."
		- p quote
			Any value can implicitly convert to a non-empty option containing it.
			So any {nat} can implicitly convert to a {type "nat?"} .

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				empty nat? = ()
				log empty
				non-empty nat? = 1
				log non-empty

		- aside "Implicit conversion and calls",
			- p quote
				Keen does implicit conversion only as necessary.
				When two overloaded functions could match the expected type,
				but only does so without implicit conversion, Keen prefers that one.
			- p quote
				When the expected type is {type "nat?"}, {parens} prefers the {fn-new} returning an option,
				not the {fn-new} returning a {nat}.
			- p quote
				If you do want a non-empty option containing a new {nat}, you could write
				{code "()"}{colon-colon}{nat}. (Though in the case of {nat}, it could just be written as {num-0}.)

		- p "Options are useful for functions that might not have a result:"

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log half 9
				log half 10

			half nat?(a nat)
				if is-even a
					a / 2

		- aside-small quote
			Remember that when the {else_} branch is omitted, it calls {parens}.
			{br}
			In this case, it means that {function "half"} returns an empty option for odd numbers.

	- section "\"if\" and options",
		- p quote
			Options implicitly convert to {bool}.
			{br}
			If you test an option in an {if_} condition, then inside the {if_} body,
			it will implicitly convert to its (non-optional) inner value.
		- p quote
			The below example uses {function "first"} from the standard library,
			which returns the first element in an array if one exists.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log describe ()
				log describe (1, 2, 3)

			describe string(a nat[])
				x nat? = first a
				if x
					"Array starting with \{x\}"
				else
					"Empty array"

		- p "The local can be introduced inside the {if_}."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log describe ()
				log describe (1, 2, 3)

			describe string(a nat[])
				if x = first a
					"Array starting with \{x\}"
				else
					"Empty array"

		- p quote
			As with {if_}, if the left side of an {and_} expression is an option,
			it is implicitly converted to a non-option on the right side.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log exists-and-is-even ()
				log exists-and-is-even 1
				log exists-and-is-even 2

			exists-and-is-even bool(x nat?)
				x and is-even x

		- p "You can even introduce a local in the left side of an {and_} expression."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log starts-the-same (), ()
				log starts-the-same (1, 2), (2, 3)
				log starts-the-same (1, 2), (1, 3)

			starts-the-same bool(a nat[], b nat[])
				(x = first a) and (y = first b) and x == y

		- p quote
			You can also test record fields. This won't work on mutable fields.
			{br}
			It also works with any function with an immutable argument.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a r = 10,
				if a.x
					# '+' doesn't work on 'nat?', but 'a.x' is converted to 'nat'
					log a.x + 1
				if a.half-x
					log a.half-x + 1

			r record(x nat?)
			half-x nat?(a r) = if a.x then a.x / 2

	- section "Option operations",
		- p quote
			{bang} is a suffix operator that forces an option.
			This gets the inner value or throws an exception if the option is empty.
			{br}
			If the {bang} comes after a function name as in {function "half"}{bang}{sp}{num-4},
			it forces the function's return value.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log (half 4)!
				# Equivalent to above:
				log half! 4
			half nat?(a nat)
				if is-even a then a / 2

		- p quote
			{a-or-b} is analogous to the boolean {keyword "or"}.
			{br}
			It first evaluates {var-a}. If that is a non-empty option, it is returned.
			Otherwise, it evaluates {var-b}.
			{br}
			In other words, {a-or-b}{sp}{or_}{sp}{code "..."} evaluates options in sequence
			and returns the first non-empty one.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat? = ()
				b nat? = 1
				c nat? = 2
				log (a or a)
				log (a or b)
				log (b or c)
				log (a or a or a or a or c)

		- aside-small quote
			If the expected type is {bool}, then {a-or-b} will convert both arguments to booleans.

		- p quote
			{a-else-b} is like {a-or-b}, but expects {var-b} to be non-optional.
			{br}
			While {a-or-b} takes two options of the same type, {a-else-b} only takes an option on the left.
			{br}
			So the {else_} expression is non-optional, since it always has {var-b} to fall back on.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat? = ()
				b nat? = 1
				log (a else 1)
				log (b else 2)

	- section "Optional calls",
		- p quote
			{keyword "?"}{fn-foo} calls {fn-foo} if all of its arguments are non-empty options.
			{br}
			Otherwise it skips the call and returns an empty option.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log ?foo 1.half, 4.half
				log ?foo 2.half, 3.half
				log ?foo 2.half, 4.half

			foo nat(a nat, b nat)
				a + b

			half nat?(a nat)
				if is-even a then a / 2

		- p "{keyword "?"}{fn-foo} can also return {void} if that is the expected type."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				?log 9.half # does nothing
				?log 10.half

			half nat?(a nat)
				if is-even a then a / 2

		- p "This also works with dot-calls: {var-x}{dot}{keyword "?"}{fn-foo}"
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				9.half.?log # does nothing
				10.half.?log

			half nat?(a nat)
				if is-even a then a / 2

		- p "It works for {fn-subscript} calls too:"
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat[]? = ()
				index nat? = 0
				log a?[index]
				b nat[]? = 1, 2, 3
				log b?[index]

	- section "Useful option functions",
		- p "{std-function "keen/option", "from-option"} converts an option to any collection."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log from-option::nat[] half 1
				log from-option::nat[] half 2
				# Works well with '--'
				log
					:: nat[]
					-- from-option half 3 # adds nothing
					-- from-option half 4 # adds '2'

			half nat?(a nat)
				if is-even a then a / 2

-a-or-b html[]()
	"{var-a}{sp}{or_}{sp}{var-b}"
-a-else-b html[]()
	"{var-a}{sp}{else_}{sp}{var-b}"
